[Music]  our series a more perfect union aims to  show what unites us as americans  is far greater than what divides us now  two-thirds of college-educated  millennials live and work in just  52 u.s metro areas this morning we  introduce you to  lead for america a program designed to  bring young talent back home  after college to help build up their  under-resourced communities  adriana diaz spoke with one of the  program's founders and three of his  fellows  about what homecoming means to them  the quiet of wilson north carolina is  only interrupted  when the outside world rolls through  i want to get you up here this is home  for 25 year old dante pittman  and the last place he thought he'd be  after going to unc  chapel hill and working as the state  attorney general's assistant in raleigh  most of the folks that i polled said i  should stay right where i was  but thank goodness for good family who  said you need to come on back home  he did to work for the city of wilson  thanks to lead for america  a non-profit that provides college grads  with two-year fellowships  in their home towns i feel like every  day i have the opportunity to change  someone's life our generation wants to  have  a tremendous impact on our communities  in my opinion the best way to do that is  to go back to where you started forget  about that shiny big city  yeah well you know i first time i went  to new york i saw a 10 pound rat so it's  not that shiny  [Laughter]  25 year old benja krauss all right see  you  soon helped create lead for america so  that hometowns everywhere  can hold on to homegrown talent that is  amazing  her family's been in small town wauseeka  minnesota for  six generations what is the impact of  the brain drain that happens when  you know people go off to college and  then they don't come back  there's a material cost to what happens  when there's brain drain rate  we see rural hospitals closing schools  unable to find teachers  i think that also sends this feeling  that there's not much hope  that this place isn't one that that is  appealing for bright people  the two-year-old program has placed  almost a hundred fellows and jobs  back home that includes small towns  rural lands tribal communities  and also economically distressed urban  neighborhoods  neighborhoods like north st louis where  23 year old  trayvon latimer grew up it was a place  that i had a heart for and wanted to see  change but  didn't necessarily have the tools  i did spend a lot of my childhood in  ferguson missouri i definitely thought a  lot about you know inequality those  issues  after college in chicago latimer came  home to work on those issues  with the fellowship supporting small and  minority owned businesses  the federal stimulus at goodell passed  in march a lot of the money for small  businesses came through our department  so  i was able to help divvy out almost 5  million dollars to  small businesses across the region what  kind of difference can someone like you  make who's actually from the community  if you have people who understand where  they're from and understand the issues  more than anybody more than any like  policy expert because they live in day  to day you will see real change  i just got a ten thousand dollar grant  the fond du lac tribal nation in  minnesota  is alexandra houchin's home with leed  she's expanding food sustainability  for her tribe what does it mean to you  that through this fellowship you can  stay home and you can do the work you  want to do  it means everything it's i just feel  really  blessed to feel so believed in and it  keeps me motivated  thank you back in wilson pittman got a  promotion  he's now the city's human relations  director  i can tell you that being in charge of a  department at 25  is not something that i thought i was  prepared for and it's certainly not  normal  but it was possible because he came home  for cbs this morning adriana diaz  chicago so you can go home again that's  right  we take exception dante pittman we think  you're great but was it rat really ten  pounds  more than eight yes i was gonna say i  want to defend our new york city rats  they're actually very friendly  but it does show you you can go home  again and you can thrive  i'm finding people are having a newer  appreciation too for going back home  yeah and two years is a good amount of  time if you want people to stay a lot  can happen in two years  meet somebody you get a routine down you  might not leave at the end of it yeah  and i truly feel like i'm living the  dream here in the big city but my heart  is in seattle washington where i was  raised so often times i think about what  i could be doing in the communities  that i was raised in um but sometimes  you have to leave your city i think  that's true take over  the world to appreciate it and also  learn a little bit more because a little  bit more worldly and then you can come  back and provide more  you're bringing back you're bringing  home more well you're still taking over  the world part  mr sportscaster thank you yeah i just  think that's so cool

